//
// A file asserts a PASS-1 error and a PASS-2 error at once, and runs its remaining cells.
//
// `Parser::parse` runs pass 2 only when pass 1 finished clean, so ONE pass-1 error silenced every
// `!first_pass` diagnostic in the same file — `Unknown variable`, the const/ref checks, match
// exhaustiveness, the whole N-Store family. A file could therefore assert pass-1 errors OR
// pass-2 errors and never both, and an `@EXPECT_ERROR` for a masked one could never match. That
// is the second reason the `<n>b-…` split exists, and it is why `102`/`102b` are two files whose
// headers each explain the other.
//
// The parser is unchanged. loft#1242's peeling is what removes the masking: the cell carrying the
// pass-1 error is blanked, the next round reaches pass 2, and the declarations are checked
// against the UNION of every round's diagnostics. Measured directly while writing this — with
// both cells present the compiler reports only the pass-1 error; delete that cell and the pass-2
// error appears.
//
// ⚠ The order of the two declarations below is not the order they are PRODUCED in, and that is
// the point: the pass-2 one does not exist until the pass-1 cell is gone. A file that could only
// ever see one of them would pass with either line removed, so both are load-bearing.
//
// The third cell runs, which is what says the peeling did not simply swallow the file.
//
// @falsified-at: 1edba23e — MEASURED by hand; the channel is the harness, not the program.
//   `make falsify` compares two BINARIES on one guard and the change is in `tests/wrap.rs`, so
//   both answer identically. On the parent this file fails: the pass-2 declaration is reported
//   `expected error not emitted`, because pass 1 stopped the parse before that diagnostic could
//   be produced. With the fix both match and `1242b RAN` prints.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Misplaced '_' in number literal
// @EXPECT_ERROR: comprehension body must produce a value
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

// PASS 1 — the lexer refuses the literal before any body is re-parsed.
fn test_1242b_a_pass_1_error() {
  x1242b = 1__0;
  assert(x1242b == 10, "unreached — pass 1 refuses this cell");
}

// PASS 2 — `!first_pass` code, unreachable while the cell above stops the parse.
fn test_1242b_a_pass_2_error() {
  v1242b = [for i1242b in 0..3 { }];
  assert(len(v1242b) == 3, "unreached — pass 2 refuses this cell");
}

// …and a cell that neither touches still runs.
fn test_1242b_a_surviving_cell_runs() {
  w1242b: vector<integer> = [];
  w1242b += [5];
  w1242b += [6];
  assert(len(w1242b) == 2, "a surviving cell runs after two peels: {len(w1242b)}");
  println("1242b RAN");
}
